PZDOOM patch 0001 — int-sized boolean (root cause of the status-bar crash)

Upstream doomgeneric 3b1d530 defines boolean as 'unsigned char' whenever
<stdbool.h> is in scope, and doomtype.h itself includes <stdbool.h> on
non-Windows. id's engine assumes boolean is int-sized (st_stuff.c binds the
arms widgets to (int *) &plyr->weaponowned[i+1]); with a 1-byte boolean that
read 4 adjacent flags as one int (257, 65793, ...) and indexed far outside
arms[][] -> SIGSEGV during demo playback as weapons were picked up. On
Windows only some translation units saw <stdbool.h> (mixed struct layouts).
Applies on top of ozkl/doomgeneric @ 3b1d53020373b502035d7d48dede645a7c429feb.

diff --git a/doomgeneric/doomtype.h b/doomgeneric/doomtype.h
index 7acaa49..21dd9f2 100644
--- a/doomgeneric/doomtype.h
+++ b/doomgeneric/doomtype.h
@@ -64,9 +64,19 @@
 
 #if defined(__cplusplus) || defined(__bool_true_false_are_defined)
 
-// Use builtin bool type with C++.
-
-typedef unsigned char boolean;
+// PZDOOM: int, not unsigned char.
+//
+// Upstream (cba6149/3b1d530) made boolean a 1-byte type whenever <stdbool.h>
+// is in scope. On non-Windows this very header includes <stdbool.h>, so every
+// translation unit got a 1-byte boolean, while id's engine code assumes
+// boolean is int-sized: st_stuff.c binds the status-bar arms widgets to
+// (int *) &plyr->weaponowned[i+1], which then read four adjacent booleans as
+// one int (e.g. 0x0101 = 257) and indexed far outside arms[][] -> segfault
+// ("demo crash"). On Windows only some TUs saw <stdbool.h>, giving mixed
+// 1-byte/4-byte layouts of shared structs and globals. An int-sized boolean
+// restores the original ABI everywhere and matches the enum branch below.
+
+typedef int boolean;
 
 #else
 
